          "pull_request": {
            "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/mswjs/interceptors/pulls/863",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/mswjs/interceptors/pull/863",
            "diff_url": "https://github.com/mswjs/interceptors/pull/863.diff",
            "patch_url": "https://github.com/mswjs/interceptors/pull/863.patch",
            "merged_at": null
          },
          "body": "- Related to https://github.com/mswjs/msw/actions/runs/37751445963/job/113225391936\r\n- Related to https://github.com/nodejs/undici/issues/5981\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Do not merge\r\n\r\nThis isn't worth the trouble. Once https://github.com/nodejs/undici/issues/5981 is merged and published, bumping the Node.js 26 version will resolve this issue. \r\n\r\n## Changes\r\n\r\n  - Node fetch interceptor is no longer socket-based. Since #770 it rode on the shared NodeHttpRequestSource: undici\r\n    dispatched to a wrapped socket, the raw bytes were parsed back into a Request, and the request event fired in\r\n    whatever async context undici's connection pool happened to be in. Now globalThis.fetch is patched directly, same\r\n    model as web.ts: Request is built from the caller's args, request event fires synchronously-ish in the caller's\r\n    frame, passthrough is realFetch(request.clone(), init) with dispatcher and all other init forwarded verbatim.\r\n  - No shared code paths changed in behavior for other interceptors. forward-events.ts untouched. ClientRequest/Http\r\n    interceptors still socket-based. Fetch-only utils (follow-redirect, create-network-error, clone-response property\r\n    descriptors) gained spec-alignment fixes that web.ts also benefits from.\r\n\r\n## Potential impact\r\n\r\n  - Async context is caller-owned. Fixes the Node 26 flake root-cause: undici 8's https protocol probe drains queued\r\n    requests in the first request's ALS frame. Any user relying on ALS inside request listeners (msw boundary(),\r\n    per-request logging) now works regardless of undici version.\r\n  - Listeners see the request as constructed, not as sent on the wire. credentials reflects init, connection: upgrade\r\n    and other headers undici would reject reach listeners first, no OPTIONS preflight events for http-polyfill fetch\r\n    (happy-dom). Mocked Response.type is default. Response listeners see decompressed bodies.\r\n  - Passthrough clones once more. Body is read twice at most (listener clone + passthrough clone). Memory-leak-style\r\n    tests counting clone() calls (msw many-request-handlers*) shift by one. Streaming request bodies on passthrough are\r\n    consumed through the clone, not replayed from raw bytes.\r\n  - Double handling risk. FetchInterceptor + ClientRequestInterceptor in one BatchInterceptor would previously dedupe\r\n    at the socket; now a passed-through fetch over an http-based polyfill is guarded by requestContext.run({ initiator\r\n    }), but native undici fetch is only seen by the fetch interceptor. Users mixing both see each request once, from\r\n    the fetch side only.\r\n  - Error surface matches undici. Rejections are TypeError('fetch failed', { cause }), abort rejects with the signal\r\n    reason, mocked 1xx rejects, HEAD responses are body-less to the caller.\r\n  - Undici decoupling. No symbol access, no dispatcher snapshotting; survives undici internals changes. Trade-off:\r\n    undici's pre-dispatch validation no longer runs before listeners (msw ws.upgrade test needs its expectation\r\n    adjusted).\r\n\r\n## Implications\r\n\r\nThis does have serious implications on the interception:\r\n\r\n- Interception now happens _before_ Undici, not within. It now misses any request validation, default headers, redirect handling, etc. That's a huge loss.\r\n- Fetch redirects now listen our internal logic instead of relying entirely on Undici.",
